   "prompt": "The increment of reserved is atomic. Why could two concurrent reservations on PostgreSQL still over-reserve?",
   "choices": [
    {
     "text": "Both can pass the earlier availability read before either increments",
     "evidence": [
      "src/server/modules/orders/orders.service.ts"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Because the 72-hour reservation expiry releases stock too early",
     "evidence": []
    },
    {
     "text": "They cannot: the interactive transaction serializes all reservations",
     "evidence": []
    },
    {
     "text": "Because Prisma's increment operation is not atomic on PostgreSQL",
     "evidence": []
    }
   ],
   "check": {
    "type": "quiz",
    "itemKey": "seed:quests:organized-study-atlas-fulfillment/quiz/33930012cc93ecf5",
    "grading": "choice",
    "answerKey": 0,
    "choices": [
     "Both can pass the earlier availability read before either increments",
     "Because the 72-hour reservation expiry releases stock too early",
     "They cannot: the interactive transaction serializes all reservations",
     "Because Prisma's increment operation is not atomic on PostgreSQL"
    ]
   },
   "explanation": "Check-then-act: SQLite's single-writer lock limits interleaving locally, but on PostgreSQL a conditional update (or row lock) must tie the check to the write."
  },
